/* Wave selection. A locked wave is a disabled row, not a hidden one - the
 * campaign's shape should be visible before it is playable.
 *
 * THE ROW ITSELF IS GONE FROM THIS FILE. `.campaign-row`'s fill, border,
 * radius, padding, margin and type were a line-by-line re-derivation of
 * `.option-row`, which now draws the row; the screen's own title rule went
 * with the title, into the scaffold's header. What is left is the two things
 * neither the frame nor the component can know: what LOCKED looks like on
 * this list, and what CLEARED does.
 *
 * `__waves` LOST ITS ONLY DECLARATION IN PHASE 9 TASK 14 AND THE RULE WENT
 * WITH IT. It set `flex-direction: column`, which is a VisualElement's
 * default and was therefore never doing anything; nothing changed when it
 * was removed, which is the test for a rule that was only ever restating the
 * layout engine. The element itself stays - `Bind` clears and refills it by
 * name, and it is now the SectionCard body's one child.
 *
 * A SCREEN CAN STYLE ITS COMPONENTS' INSIDES, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE OF
 * StatCell.uss's note and not a contradiction of it. This sheet is attached
 * at this screen's root, so it reaches every descendant - including the
 * OptionRows added to `#waves`. What a COMPONENT's sheet cannot reach is its
 * own PARENT, which is upward rather than downward.
 */
.campaign-select-view { flex-grow: 1; }

/* Unity's default theme dims a disabled element to 50% opacity, which would
 * take the row's border and the padlock with it and read as damage rather
 * than as a state. The locked treatment is stated instead: the sunk fill it
 * already has, and its words in --mute-soft. */
.campaign-row:disabled { opacity: 1; }
.campaign-row:disabled .option-row__title { color: var(--mute-soft); }
.campaign-row:disabled .option-row__detail { color: var(--mute-soft); }

/* OUT OF FLOW at the row's right edge, for both marks. `.option-row` is a
 * column of two labels, so an in-flow glyph or chip would land under them as
 * a third line; a VisualElement's default position is relative, which is what
 * an absolutely positioned child resolves against. --space-3 is the row's own
 * padding, so the mark sits on the same inset as the text.
 *
 * ONE RULE FOR THE PADLOCK AND THE CHIP because they are one idea - the
 * at-a-glance mark for a row whose state the detail line has already spelled
 * out. A row never carries both: CampaignSelectView.RowFor branches. */
.campaign-row > .icon,
.campaign-row > .chip {
    position: absolute;
    right: var(--space-3);
    top: var(--space-3);
}

/* THE CLEARED CHIP'S GREEN, IN TWO CLASSES RATHER THAN ONE. Theme.uss's
 * `.chip` sets --violet-tint and --violet-text at single-class specificity;
 * a single-class rule here would tie with it and be settled by whichever
 * sheet the panel applied last, which is the order-dependence every override
 * in this project is written to avoid (MutationBanner.uss's header states
 * it). Two classes is (0,2,0) against (0,1,0) and settles deterministically.
 *
 * `color` ON THE CHIP WORKS HERE, WHICH IS NOT TRUE OF `GenChip`. That
 * component's note records the trap: a Label INSIDE a chip takes --ink from
 * Theme's own `Label` rule, because a rule applying DIRECTLY to an element
 * beats a value inherited into it. This chip IS the Label, so the class rule
 * applies directly and wins on specificity over the type rule.
 *
 * --green-text on --green-tint is 5.10:1, which is the family's own measured
 * pairing and clears WCAG AA for small text. The chip renders at
 * --text-micro (10px) from `.chip`; that is under AA's floor for small text
 * on size alone, which is true of every chip in the app and is why bible
 * 10.4 requires the word as well - "Cleared" is in the detail line above it
 * at --text-secondary. */
.campaign-select-view .campaign-select-view__cleared {
    background-color: var(--green-tint);
    color: var(--green-text);
}
